## Why This Is Asked

Interacting across tenants requires real-time policy-compliant routing. The candidate must design a routing/policy layer that respects ABAC, handles sensitive content, and balances safety with latency, all while exposing auditable decisions.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt intent extraction and sensitive-term detection
- ABAC policy evaluation against user attributes
- Model variant selection under policy constraints
- Auditing: fingerprinting, latency, policy_score, route trace
- Lightweight prototype with mock models for realism

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt, tenant) {
  const intents = /summarize|export|analyze|decrypt/.test(prompt) ? 'specialized' : 'general';
  const sensitive = /PII|password|secret/.test(prompt);
  const policy = (tenant.policies || []).find(p => p.action === 'route');
  const model = policy && policy.allows && !sensitive ? 'fast-safe' : 'strict';
  const fingerprint = hash(prompt + tenant.id);
  return { finalPrompt: prompt, model, fingerprint };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle policy updates and cache invalidation?
- What metrics would you collect to detect model drift after routing decisions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?

